        "note_tweet": {
          "entities": {},
          "text": "Economics remains a cottage-industry model to this day, especially in its theoretical branch. Even after winning the Nobel Prize, many scholars continue to personally sit at their desks writing papers and deriving models. This kind of work, much like mathematics, is inherently difficult to scale up or industrialize. As a result, the research workload has kept increasing since the beginning of one’s career. At the same time, as reputation and achievements grow, demands from governments, commercial organizations, and other institutions also multiply. For ambitious and high-achieving individuals who are unwilling to sacrifice their academic output to handle these additional responsibilities, the only option left is to continuously compress their rest and leisure time."
